A class is a blueprint: it declares what *kind* of state every object built from it will carry (its fields) and what behavior every such object will support (its methods) -- but the class itself is not a thing you can use directly; you need actual objects, created from that blueprint with `new`, before there's any state to work with at all.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private String owner;
    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(String owner, double balance) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

`BankAccount` describes the *shape* every account will have -- an owner and a balance -- but declaring the class compiles no accounts into existence. `new BankAccount("Amy", 100.0)` is what actually allocates a fresh object on the heap, runs the constructor against it, and hands back a reference to that specific object. Create two accounts and you get two entirely separate objects, each with its own independent copy of `owner` and `balance` -- mutating one's balance has zero effect on the other's, because 'instance fields' means exactly that: each *instance* (each object) carries its own copy, distinct from the class itself and from every other instance. This is worth contrasting directly with `static` fields (briefly touched on when you learned why `main` has to be `static`): a `static` field belongs to the class itself, shared by every instance, with exactly one copy total no matter how many objects exist -- an instance field is the default, ordinary case, and `static` is the deliberate exception you reach for only when data genuinely belongs to the class as a whole rather than to any one object.

A constructor looks like a method with no return type, sharing the class's exact name, and it runs automatically, exactly once, every time `new` creates an object -- its entire job is to get the new object into a valid starting state before anyone else can touch it. If you write no constructor at all, Java silently supplies a no-argument default constructor that does nothing beyond giving fields their default values (`0`, `false`, `null`) -- but the moment you write *any* constructor yourself, that free default disappears entirely; if you still want a no-argument way to create the object, you have to write one explicitly.

Classes commonly need more than one way to be constructed, and Java lets you overload constructors exactly like methods -- same name (mandatory, since it's always the class name), different parameter lists:

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private String owner;
    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(String owner, double balance) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public BankAccount(String owner) {
        this(owner, 0.0);   // constructor chaining -- delegates to the constructor above
    }
}
```

`this(owner, 0.0)` is **constructor chaining**: it calls another constructor of the *same* class, and it must be the very first statement in the constructor body if it's used at all -- Java enforces this so that an object's initialization always resolves down to exactly one 'real' path of field assignments, rather than each constructor duplicating the same assignment logic. Without chaining, adding a validation rule (say, rejecting a negative opening balance) later would mean hunting down and fixing every constructor that assigns `balance` separately; with chaining, only the one constructor that actually does the assignment needs to change.

Notice `this.owner = owner;` in the first constructor: the parameter is intentionally named the same as the field it initializes, which is idiomatic and reads clearly at the call site, but it creates a genuine naming collision inside the constructor body. `this.owner` explicitly means 'the field belonging to the object being constructed'; a bare `owner` on its own refers to the *parameter*, which shadows the field for the remainder of the constructor. Writing `owner = owner;` by mistake (forgetting `this.`) compiles without error -- it's legal Java -- and does nothing useful at all: it assigns the parameter to itself, and the field silently keeps its default value (`null`, for a `String`). This specific bug is common enough that it's worth developing the habit of writing `this.fieldName = fieldName;` reflexively whenever a constructor or setter parameter intentionally shares a name with the field it sets.

Making a field `private` is necessary for encapsulation, but it is not, by itself, sufficient -- a genuinely common mistake is writing a getter that returns a direct reference to a mutable field, which hands the caller a way to mutate the object's internal state anyway, completely bypassing every rule the class thought it was enforcing.

```java
public class Team {
    private List<String> members;

    public Team() {
        this.members = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public List<String> getMembers() {
        return members;   // leaks the actual internal list!
    }
}

Team team = new Team();
team.getMembers().add("Zoe");   // mutates Team's PRIVATE internal state from outside!
```

`members` is `private`, and there's no public setter for it -- it looks encapsulated. But `getMembers()` returns the exact same `List` object that lives inside `team`, not a copy, and lists are mutable -- so any caller holding that returned reference can call `.add()`, `.remove()`, or `.clear()` on it and directly alter `team`'s internal state, with the `Team` class having no idea it happened and no chance to validate or reject it. This is a direct application of the reference-vs-primitive distinction from earlier in the course: returning a reference-type field from a getter hands out a *live connection* to the object's own internal state, not an independent, safe-to-look-at snapshot of it.

The fix is a **defensive copy**: return a new list containing the same elements, so the caller can look at the data (or even mutate their own copy freely) without any of that reaching back into `team`'s actual internals: `return new ArrayList<>(members);`. The same trap applies to arrays and to any other mutable object type returned from a getter -- primitives and immutable types (like `String`, or numeric wrapper types) don't have this problem at all, because there's no way to 'mutate in place' a value that's immutable; the danger is specifically about handing out live references to mutable objects.

