`class Dog extends Animal` declares `Dog` a **subclass** of `Animal` (equivalently, `Animal` is `Dog`'s **superclass**) -- and it means every `Dog` object automatically has, in addition to whatever `Dog` itself declares, all the non-private fields and methods `Animal` declares too, without `Dog` having to re-declare or re-implement any of them.

```java
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating.");
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " says woof!");
    }
}

Dog rex = new Dog("Rex");
rex.eat();    // inherited from Animal -- 'Rex is eating.'
rex.bark();   // declared directly on Dog -- 'Rex says woof!'
```

`Dog` never writes an `eat()` method of its own, and yet `rex.eat()` works -- it's calling the exact `eat()` method body defined in `Animal`, inherited unchanged. Note the field is declared `protected`, not `private`: `protected` means 'accessible within this class, and within any subclass, but not from unrelated outside code' -- exactly the access level that lets `Dog`'s methods reference `name` directly. Had `name` been `private` in `Animal`, `Dog` would still inherit the *field itself* (every `Dog` object still has that storage), but `Dog`'s own code could not refer to `name` directly by that name -- it would have to go through whatever public or protected accessor `Animal` provides, exactly the same as any other outside code would. This is a genuinely important nuance: private members are inherited in the sense that the storage exists on the object, but they are not *accessible* to the subclass's own code.

Constructors are the one thing `extends` explicitly does *not* hand down: `Dog` does not automatically get `Animal`'s constructor the way it gets `Animal`'s methods. Every subclass must define its own constructors, and -- this is the part that surprises people -- **every subclass constructor's very first action, explicit or not, is a call to a superclass constructor.** If you don't write one yourself, Java silently inserts a call to the superclass's no-argument constructor, `super()`, as the first line. If the superclass has no accessible no-argument constructor (because, like `Animal` above, it only defines one that takes a `name`), that implicit insertion fails to compile, and you're required to write the `super(...)` call explicitly, matching one of the superclass's actual constructors.

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);       // must be the FIRST statement -- calls Animal's constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }
}
```

The reasoning is structural, not a stylistic rule: by the time `Dog`'s own constructor body starts doing anything with inherited state (like `name`), that state needs to already be properly initialized -- and the only code that knows how to correctly initialize `Animal`'s own fields is `Animal`'s constructor. So Java enforces, as a hard compile-time rule, that superclass initialization always completes before subclass initialization even begins. This cascades all the way up an inheritance chain: constructing a `Dog` first fully runs `Object`'s (implicit) constructor, then `Animal`'s, then finally `Dog`'s own body -- top of the hierarchy down, even though you only ever wrote `new Dog(...)`.

A subclass can **override** an inherited method by redeclaring it with the exact same signature (name, parameter types, and a compatible return type) and a new body -- this replaces the inherited behavior for objects of that subclass, and it's the mechanism behind what's often called **run-time polymorphism**: which version of the method actually runs is decided by the object's *real* type, not by the declared type of the reference variable pointing at it.

```java
public class Animal {
    public String makeSound() {
        return "...";
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public String makeSound() {
        return "Woof!";
    }
}

Animal a = new Dog();       // declared type Animal, ACTUAL type Dog
System.out.println(a.makeSound());   // 'Woof!' -- not '...'
```

`a` is declared as `Animal`, but the object it actually refers to is a `Dog` -- and `makeSound()` dispatches based on that real, run-time type, printing `"Woof!"`. This is precisely the opposite mechanism from overloading (covered earlier in the course): overloading is resolved at *compile time*, purely from the declared types at the call site; overriding is resolved at *run time*, from the actual object's type, and this is exactly why the two are sometimes called compile-time versus run-time polymorphism. The `@Override` annotation above the method is not required by the compiler to make overriding work -- but it's a genuinely valuable safety net: it tells the compiler 'I intend this to override something,' and if there's a typo in the method name, a mismatched parameter type, or the superclass method simply doesn't exist, `@Override` turns what would otherwise be a silent new, unrelated method (accidental overloading instead of overriding) into a compile error you'll actually notice.

Inside an overriding method, `super.methodName(...)` calls the *superclass's* version of that method explicitly, rather than the overriding version -- useful when a subclass wants to *extend* the inherited behavior rather than fully *replace* it.

```java
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public String describe() {
        return "An animal named " + name;
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return super.describe() + ", a " + breed;   // builds on Animal's version, doesn't replace it
    }
}
```

Without `super.describe()`, `Dog`'s override would have to fully re-derive `"An animal named " + name` itself, duplicating logic that already exists correctly in `Animal` -- and if `Animal`'s version of that logic ever changes, `Dog`'s duplicated copy would silently drift out of sync. `super.describe()` calls exactly one specific version -- the superclass's -- bypassing the normal run-time dispatch for that one call; it does not, itself, trigger any further overriding lookup. This is genuinely different from calling `this.describe()` from inside `Dog`, which would infinitely recurse back into `Dog`'s own override, since `this` always dispatches based on the real run-time type.

