An `abstract class` is a class that's explicitly incomplete on purpose: it can declare regular, fully-implemented methods exactly like any class, but it can also declare **abstract methods** -- a signature with no body at all, ending in a semicolon -- that every concrete (non-abstract) subclass is *required* to implement. Because it's incomplete by design, Java simply refuses to let you write `new` against an abstract class directly, even if it has a public constructor -- there's a real, structural gap in it (the missing method bodies), and instantiating it would mean having an object with methods that don't actually do anything.

```java
public abstract class Shape {
    protected String name;

    public Shape(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public abstract double area();   // no body -- every concrete subclass must supply one

    public String describe() {       // ordinary, fully-implemented method -- inherited as-is
        return name + " has area " + area();
    }
}

public class Circle extends Shape {
    private double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        super("Circle");
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    public double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

// Shape s = new Shape("generic");   // compile error: Shape is abstract; cannot be instantiated
Shape c = new Circle(3.0);          // fine -- Circle is concrete and implements area()
System.out.println(c.describe());   // 'Circle has area 28.27...'
```

Notice `describe()` calls `area()` even though `Shape` itself has no idea what that returns -- and this is exactly the point of an abstract class: it can write real, shared logic (`describe()`) that depends on behavior it deliberately leaves to subclasses to fill in, and that logic gets to assume every concrete subclass genuinely has a working `area()`, because the compiler enforces that guarantee. If `Circle` failed to override `area()`, it would itself have to be declared `abstract` too (deferring the obligation one level further down), or the code simply fails to compile.

An `interface` declares a set of methods a class promises to provide, with (traditionally) no implementation and no instance state (fields) of its own at all -- it's a contract about *what a class can do*, completely decoupled from *what a class is* in an inheritance sense. A class uses `implements` to adopt an interface, and, critically, a class can `implement` as many interfaces as it needs, even though it can `extend` only one class.

```java
public interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

public interface Swimmable {
    void swim();
}

public class Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable {
    @Override
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Duck flying");
    }

    @Override
    public void swim() {
        System.out.println("Duck swimming");
    }
}
```

`Duck` has no inheritance relationship at all to whatever other class might also implement `Flyable` (an `Airplane`, say) -- interfaces model 'can-do' capability, entirely separate from 'is-a' ancestry. This is exactly why Java allows implementing several interfaces at once but extending only one class: multiple unrelated capabilities genuinely can combine on one class without ambiguity, because there's no shared implementation or state to reconcile between them -- whereas multiple inheritance of actual class *implementation* (fields, method bodies) runs into real, unresolvable ambiguity if two superclasses both defined a conflicting field or method body (the classic 'diamond problem'), which is exactly why Java sidesteps it by disallowing multiple class inheritance altogether.

Since Java 8, an interface can include **default methods**: methods with a real body, marked `default`, that implementing classes inherit automatically and are free to override or simply leave as-is. This closes a real gap that pure abstract-method-only interfaces had: adding a *new* abstract method to an interface used by many existing classes would break every one of them (they'd suddenly be missing a required implementation) -- a default method instead ships a working fallback, so existing implementers keep compiling unchanged.

```java
public interface Greeter {
    String name();

    default String greet() {
        return "Hello, " + name() + "!";
    }
}

public class Person implements Greeter {
    private String personName;

    public Person(String personName) {
        this.personName = personName;
    }

    @Override
    public String name() {
        return personName;
    }
    // no need to implement greet() -- the default is used as-is
}

Person p = new Person("Priya");
System.out.println(p.greet());   // 'Hello, Priya!'
```

`Person` only ever implements `name()`; `greet()` comes for free from the interface's default, and it works correctly because it calls `name()` -- which, at run time, dispatches to whatever concrete implementation is actually providing it, exactly like any other method call. Worth being precise about the limit here: default methods still cannot carry instance *state* -- an interface still has no fields to store per-object data in; a default method can only compute from the abstract methods (and any other default methods) the interface itself declares.

